===============
Grammar Parsing
===============

Grammars can be parsed from strings:

    >>> from nltk import CFG
    >>> grammar = CFG.fromstring("""
    ... S -> NP VP
    ... PP -> P NP
    ... NP -> Det N | NP PP
    ... VP -> V NP | VP PP
    ... Det -> 'a' | 'the'
    ... N -> 'dog' | 'cat'
    ... V -> 'chased' | 'sat'
    ... P -> 'on' | 'in'
    ... """)
    >>> grammar
    <Grammar with 14 productions>
    >>> grammar.start()
    S
    >>> grammar.productions()
    [S -> NP VP, PP -> P NP, NP -> Det N, NP -> NP PP, VP -> V NP, VP -> VP PP,
    Det -> 'a', Det -> 'the', N -> 'dog', N -> 'cat', V -> 'chased', V -> 'sat',
    P -> 'on', P -> 'in']

Probabilistic CFGs:

    >>> from nltk import PCFG
    >>> toy_pcfg1 = PCFG.fromstring("""
    ... S -> NP VP [1.0]
    ... NP -> Det N [0.5] | NP PP [0.25] | 'John' [0.1] | 'I' [0.15]
    ... Det -> 'the' [0.8] | 'my' [0.2]
    ... N -> 'man' [0.5] | 'telescope' [0.5]
    ... VP -> VP PP [0.1] | V NP [0.7] | V [0.2]
    ... V -> 'ate' [0.35] | 'saw' [0.65]
    ... PP -> P NP [1.0]
    ... P -> 'with' [0.61] | 'under' [0.39]
    ... """)

Chomsky Normal Form grammar (Test for bug 474)

    >>> g = CFG.fromstring("VP^<TOP> -> VBP NP^<VP-TOP>")
    >>> g.productions()[0].lhs()
    VP^<TOP>

Grammars can contain both empty strings and empty productions:

    >>> from nltk.grammar import CFG
    >>> from nltk.parse.generate import generate
    >>> grammar = CFG.fromstring("""
    ... S -> A B
    ... A -> 'a'
    ... # An empty string:
    ... B -> 'b' | ''
    ... """)
    >>> list(generate(grammar))
    [['a', 'b'], ['a', '']]
    >>> grammar = CFG.fromstring("""
    ... S -> A B
    ... A -> 'a'
    ... # An empty production:
    ... B -> 'b' |
    ... """)
    >>> list(generate(grammar))
    [['a', 'b'], ['a']]

Grammars with mixed rules can be converted into Chomsky Normal Form:

    >>> from nltk import CFG
    >>> grammar = CFG.fromstring("""
    ... S -> NP VP
    ... PP -> P NP
    ... NP -> NP PP P
    ... NP -> 'the' Nom | 'a' Nom
    ... VP -> V NP | VP PP
    ... Det -> 'a' | 'the'
    ... Nom -> 'dog' | 'cat'
    ... V -> 'chased' | 'sat'
    ... P -> 'on' | 'in'
    ... """)
    >>> grammar
    <Grammar with 15 productions>
    >>> grammar.chomsky_normal_form()
    <Grammar with 18 productions>

Probabilistic Chomsky normal form
--------------------------------

``PCFG.chomsky_normal_form()`` returns a PCFG. It preserves the probability
of each finite token sequence, summed over all its parses. It leaves the input
unchanged and removes rules that are unreachable from the start symbol.
For example, nonlexical unary rules are eliminated without losing their weights:

    >>> grammar = PCFG.fromstring("""
    ... S -> VP [1.0]
    ... VP -> V [1.0]
    ... V -> 'v' [0.3] | 'a' [0.7]
    ... """)
    >>> cnf = grammar.chomsky_normal_form()
    >>> cnf.productions()
    [S -> 'v' [0.3], S -> 'a' [0.7]]
    >>> cnf.start()
    S
    >>> len(grammar.productions())
    4

For a longer rule, only the first binary rule carries the original probability.
Each auxiliary rule has probability one. Auxiliaries are fresh for each rule,
including when alternatives share a prefix; terminals in longer rules receive
separate lexical auxiliaries. Thus neither shared prefixes nor terminal spelling
can accidentally combine alternatives:

    >>> grammar = PCFG.fromstring("""
    ... S -> A B C [0.4] | A B D [0.6]
    ... A -> 'a' [1.0]
    ... B -> 'b' [1.0]
    ... C -> 'c' [1.0]
    ... D -> 'd' [1.0]
    ... """)
    >>> cnf = grammar.chomsky_normal_form()
    >>> cnf.is_chomsky_normal_form()
    True
    >>> from nltk.parse import InsideChartParser
    >>> parser = InsideChartParser(cnf)
    >>> sum(tree.prob() for tree in parser.parse(['a', 'b', 'c']))
    0.4
    >>> sum(tree.prob() for tree in parser.parse(['a', 'b', 'd']))
    0.6

The default ``@$@`` padding is accepted by the grammar reader. Include an
explicit start directive when serializing productions, since their order need
not identify the start symbol:

    >>> text = '% start ' + str(cnf.start()) + '\n'
    >>> text += '\n'.join(map(str, cnf.productions()))
    >>> restored = PCFG.fromstring(text)
    >>> restored.productions() == cnf.productions()
    True
    >>> restored.start() == cnf.start()
    True

Unary cycles and nontermination
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Unary elimination follows the hitting-probability construction in Appendix B,
Lemma B.13 of Etessami, Stewart and Yannakakis, `Polynomial Time Algorithms for
Multi-Type Branching Processes and Stochastic Context-Free Grammars
<https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.2374>`_. Let ``U[A,B]`` sum the probabilities of
``A -> B``, and ``B[A,gamma]`` sum the direct probabilities of each distinct
non-unary RHS ``gamma``. The desired exit weights satisfy ``X = U X + B``.

The implementation solves these equations by state elimination: sum a state's
self-loop geometrically, then substitute its remaining transitions into the
other equations. Ordered RHS tuples keep ``A B`` distinct from ``B A``.
No NumPy dependency is needed. For example:

    >>> grammar = PCFG.fromstring("""
    ... S -> A [1.0]
    ... A -> B [0.5] | 'a' [0.5]
    ... B -> A [0.25] | 'b' [0.75]
    ... """)
    >>> cnf = grammar.chomsky_normal_form()
    >>> probabilities = {p.rhs(): p.prob() for p in cnf.productions()}
    >>> round(probabilities[('a',)], 12) == round(4 / 7, 12)
    True
    >>> round(probabilities[('b',)], 12) == round(3 / 7, 12)
    True

A closed unary cycle has no finite derivations. Its incoming mass is retained
through a fresh binary nonterminal ``D -> D D [1.0]``, as in the same lemma.
For ``S -> 'x' [0.5] | A [0.5]; A -> A [1.0]``, the result still assigns
probability 0.5 to ``'x'``. Renormalizing the terminating alternative to one would
change the grammar's meaning. Binary nontermination is also allowed; local
probability sums of one do not imply termination with probability one.

Numerical behavior and scope
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The conversion uses floating-point arithmetic. Input rule probabilities must
be finite and nonnegative, with each LHS total within ``1e-12`` of one. This
is stricter than the PCFG constructor's tolerance. Materially unnormalized
inputs are rejected instead of silently changing their probabilities. When
eliminating a self-loop, its exit probability is calculated by summing the
remaining mass, which avoids cancellation in ``1 - loop_probability`` near
one. Exit totals outside the same tolerance raise an error; an individual
exit just above one due to roundoff is clipped to one. Small positive rules
are retained; there is no probability cutoff.
Floating-point underflow and accumulated rounding still limit accuracy for
extreme inputs; this is not an arbitrary-precision implementation.

Terminal isolation and binarization preserve each rule's weight by multiplying
it by ones. Summing unary paths combines all ways to reach the same ordered
RHS. Removing unreachable rules cannot change a start-symbol derivation.
Together these operations preserve finite-string probabilities in exact
arithmetic. They change tree structure and can combine distinct parses, so
original tree probabilities and Viterbi scores are not preserved.

``flexible=True`` retains nonlexical unary rules after terminal isolation and
binarization. Empty productions remain unsupported; eliminating them would
require an additional treatment of empty-string probability. The ordinary
CFG conversion retains its existing behavior apart from accepting ``@`` and
``$`` in generated nonterminal names.

``nltk/test/unit/test_pcfg_cnf.py`` checks complete distributions of small acyclic
grammars against independent leftmost derivation enumeration and a CKY inside
sum. Cyclic fixtures use hand-derived probabilities and bounded Markov walks
whose remaining mass is at most ``2**-80``. The suite also covers shared prefixes,
symbol collisions, duplicate and reordered RHSs, nontermination, small positive
probabilities, parser compatibility, and serialization for both CFG and PCFG.
